                                   "title":  "Letterboxd review of Pulse",
                                   "snippet":  "[Letterboxd Review of \"Pulse\"] SPOOKTOBER V: DEATH’S DEVICENot the most fun film dealing with deadly appliances at a home I’ve seen during this marathon, but its playful tone certainly makes it stand on its own. I wouldn’t go as far as to say it has that Amblin imprint, but there’s a little whimsy in the form of our young boy at the center of this strange, technical mystery. Technically speaking, the direction is pretty solid. There are a few moments of decent suspense. The visual effects obviously aren’t top tier, but the way they are utilized paired with the more practical “aftermaths” do well on getting the job done.Youn",
                                   "link":  "https://letterboxd.com/film/pulse/"
                               },
                               {
                                   "title":  "Letterboxd review of Pulse",
                                   "snippet":  "[Letterboxd Review of \"Pulse\"] Another classic video store VHS rental that I never got around to renting. I was always drawn to the cover, I mean look at it how could you not be? But I think the reason I never bothered is because kid me was pretty turned off by the PG-13 rating. I wanted the hard stuff or nothing at all! It turns out this is a pretty fun movie and it gets bonus points for being something a little different.Rogue electricity terrorizing the suburban home of Roxanne Hart and Joey Lawrence is something that I probably appreciate now more than I would have then anyway. It’s fairly bloodless except one scene and",
                                   "link":  "https://letterboxd.com/film/pulse/"
                               },
                               {
                                   "title":  "Letterboxd review of Pulse",
                                   "snippet":  "[Letterboxd Review of \"Pulse\"] I like to think Kiyoshi Kurosawa watched this at some point and said, \"Cool title, but I think I can do techno-horror better.\" And yes, I\u0027m aware that the original title for his film is 回路 (Kairo), which actually means circuit, but that didn\u0027t work for my dumb joke. Jozlyn wasn\u0027t feeling this one, as she couldn\u0027t get past the inherent silliness of sentient electricity being the big bad. If you, however, can bring yourself to suspend disbelief, this is an adequately charming little Poltergeist knockoff that delivers a lot of nostalgia for anyone who grew up with that kind of stuff. I actually k",
                                   "link":  "https://letterboxd.com/film/pulse/"
                               },
                               {
                                   "title":  "Letterboxd review of Pulse",
                                   "snippet":  "[Letterboxd Review of \"Pulse\"] More basic than a bag of off-brand tighty-whities, but it did make me nostalgic for the VHS horror boom of the late 80s when all you needed was some killer box art and a simple premise like \"electricity evil!\". I kinda assumed this must have been a TV movie, but it wasn\u0027t, just a straight forward pg-13 shocker, which makes sense considering the production design and budget (incredible opening title sequence, btw). I guess the most notable aspect was casting both of the older Lawrence brothers as children before they were famous. Loved hearing a 6yo Matthew Lawrence describe the opening murder",
                                   "link":  "https://letterboxd.com/film/pulse/"
